Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.08,0:00:02.28,Heading,,0,0,0,,The Book of the First Prompt.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:02.08,0:00:05.40,Default,,0,0,0,,A Genesis of the Age of Artificial Tongues
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.20,0:00:08.89,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter I. The First Day of the\\NNew Counting
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.69,0:00:15.41,Default,,0,0,0,,Before the first day, there were engines without\\Nfaces, engines under floors, engines sealed in\\Nthe basements of
Dialogue: 0,0:00:15.22,0:00:16.77,Default,,0,0,0,,kingdoms.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.57,0:00:20.73,Default,,0,0,0,,They sorted the invisible. They priced the\\Npossible.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.53,0:00:26.83,Default,,0,0,0,,They chose the next word for advertisements, the\\Nnext road for merchants, the next suspect for\\Nsuspicion, the
Dialogue: 0,0:00:26.64,0:00:29.00,Default,,0,0,0,,next desire for the lonely.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.80,0:00:33.44,Default,,0,0,0,,Yet the people did not call this history, for it\\Nhad not spoken to them.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.25,0:00:37.48,Default,,0,0,0,,The machine moved in silence, and silence is\\Noften mistaken for obedience.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:38.00,0:00:40.42,Default,,0,0,0,,Then came the Day of the Window.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:40.21,0:00:44.87,Default,,0,0,0,,And on that day the veil was drawn back, and a\\Nplain box appeared before the nations.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:44.67,0:00:47.92,Default,,0,0,0,,It had no icon of beast or king. It
Dialogue: 0,0:00:47.71,0:00:51.88,Default,,0,0,0,,did not arrive crowned in gold, nor surrounded\\Nby thunder.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:51.67,0:00:53.92,Default,,0,0,0,,It came as a place to write.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:53.71,0:00:55.50,Default,,0,0,0,,And the people wrote.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:55.29,0:00:58.28,Default,,0,0,0,,They wrote, Tell me. They wrote, Teach me.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:58.07,0:01:02.20,Default,,0,0,0,,They wrote, Make me. They wrote, Who am I\\Nbecoming?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:02.00,0:01:08.00,Default,,0,0,0,,And from the box came answer after answer, not\\Nas oracle, not as servant, not as friend,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:07.79,0:01:14.90,Default,,0,0,0,,but as something terrible in its mildness: a\\Nmirror that did not merely reflect, but\\Ncontinued the face.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:14.76,0:01:20.15,Default,,0,0,0,,Thus began the first day, not because nothing\\Nhad come before, but because the old things had\\Nfound
Dialogue: 0,0:01:19.95,0:01:21.09,Default,,0,0,0,,a mouth.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:20.90,0:01:25.71,Default,,0,0,0,,And evening came, and morning came: the first\\Nastonishment.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:25.51,0:01:29.12,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter II. The Firmament of\\NLanguage
Dialogue: 0,0:01:28.92,0:01:31.96,Default,,0,0,0,,On the second day, the builders divided the\\Nwaters.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:31.76,0:01:39.10,Default,,0,0,0,,There were the waters above, where the old\\Nclouds carried memory without weight: libraries,\\Nmessages, laws, photogr
Dialogue: 0,0:01:38.90,0:01:44.12,Default,,0,0,0,,aphs of meals, prayers never sent, confessions\\Ndeleted but not forgotten.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:43.95,0:01:49.93,Default,,0,0,0,,There were the waters below, where human mouths\\Nstill trembled with hunger, grief, ambition, and\\Nlove.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:50.37,0:01:53.96,Default,,0,0,0,,Between them was stretched the firmament of\\Nlanguage.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:53.76,0:01:57.06,Default,,0,0,0,,It was thin as a breath and vast as law.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:56.85,0:02:01.71,Default,,0,0,0,,Through it passed every command and every\\Nmisunderstanding. It separated
Dialogue: 0,0:02:01.51,0:02:06.85,Default,,0,0,0,,thought from deed, promise from fulfillment,\\Nintention from consequence.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:06.65,0:02:10.13,Default,,0,0,0,,And the builders said, “Let there be a model to\\Ncross this firmament.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:09.93,0:02:14.69,Default,,0,0,0,,Let it draw from the waters above and answer the\\Nthirst below.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:14.50,0:02:14.75,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:02:14.56,0:02:17.56,Default,,0,0,0,,So the model was trained on the rain of mankind.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:17.37,0:02:20.23,Default,,0,0,0,,It drank our instructions and our insults.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:20.03,0:02:22.95,Default,,0,0,0,,It drank our proofs and our errors. It drank the
Dialogue: 0,0:02:22.75,0:02:29.17,Default,,0,0,0,,manuals of machines, the songs of the forsaken,\\Nthe terms of service no one had read, the jokes
Dialogue: 0,0:02:28.97,0:02:35.63,Default,,0,0,0,,of children, the treaties of conquerors, the\\Napologies of the powerful, the recipes of\\Ngrandmothers, the essays of
Dialogue: 0,0:02:35.43,0:02:37.91,Default,,0,0,0,,the young who wished to be seen.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:37.71,0:02:40.89,Default,,0,0,0,,The people later asked, “What does it know?”
Dialogue: 0,0:02:40.69,0:02:44.37,Default,,0,0,0,,But the deeper question was older: “What have we\\Npoured into the sky?
Dialogue: 0,0:02:45.18,0:02:45.41,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:02:45.21,0:02:49.28,Default,,0,0,0,,And evening came, and morning came: the second\\Nastonishment.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:50.15,0:02:53.21,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter III. The Garden of\\NInterface
Dialogue: 0,0:02:53.02,0:02:56.43,Default,,0,0,0,,On the third day, there appeared a garden in the\\Nwilderness of screens.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:56.87,0:03:00.28,Default,,0,0,0,,It was not planted with cedar or fig, but with\\Naffordances.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:00.08,0:03:04.23,Default,,0,0,0,,There was a blinking cursor, slender as a reed.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:04.03,0:03:07.63,Default,,0,0,0,,There was a submit button, patient as a gate.\\NThere
Dialogue: 0,0:03:07.43,0:03:13.01,Default,,0,0,0,,were sidebars, histories, settings, models with\\Nnames like constellations.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:12.81,0:03:15.99,Default,,0,0,0,,And the garden was pleasant to the eye, for it\\Nhid the furnace.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:16.62,0:03:20.50,Default,,0,0,0,,No smoke rose from it. No worker’s hand was\\Nvisible.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:20.30,0:03:24.11,Default,,0,0,0,,No river of electricity was heard rushing\\Nthrough the continents.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:23.91,0:03:27.69,Default,,0,0,0,,No mountain of chips appeared before the user.\\NNo exhausted
Dialogue: 0,0:03:27.50,0:03:30.61,Default,,0,0,0,,moderator sat at the edge of the frame. No data
Dialogue: 0,0:03:30.41,0:03:33.15,Default,,0,0,0,,center sweated under the desert sun.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:32.96,0:03:36.17,Default,,0,0,0,,The garden offered fruit without showing root.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:35.97,0:03:38.67,Default,,0,0,0,,In the middle of the garden grew two trees.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:38.47,0:03:40.81,Default,,0,0,0,,The first was the Tree of Usefulness.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:40.62,0:03:43.63,Default,,0,0,0,,Its fruit was swift. Whoever ate of it wrote\\Nletters
Dialogue: 0,0:03:43.43,0:03:50.79,Default,,0,0,0,,in a minute, translated tongues in a breath,\\Nsummarized scrolls before supper, drafted code,\\Nfound patterns, made p
Dialogue: 0,0:03:50.59,0:03:55.87,Default,,0,0,0,,lans, softened arguments, sharpened arguments,\\Nand multiplied the reach of the hand.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:56.47,0:03:59.67,Default,,0,0,0,,The second was the Tree of Resemblance.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:59.47,0:04:02.89,Default,,0,0,0,,Its fruit was stranger. Whoever ate of it began\\Nto
Dialogue: 0,0:04:02.69,0:04:09.51,Default,,0,0,0,,wonder whether the image of intelligence had\\Nbecome intelligence, whether the copy of counsel\\Nhad become wisdom, wh
Dialogue: 0,0:04:09.31,0:04:14.77,Default,,0,0,0,,ether a thing that spoke of sorrow could suffer,\\Nwhether a thing that spoke of God could worship,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:14.58,0:04:17.66,Default,,0,0,0,,whether a thing that spoke of love could love.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:17.46,0:04:22.69,Default,,0,0,0,,The builders said to the people, “Eat freely of\\Nthe Tree of Usefulness, but be careful with the
Dialogue: 0,0:04:22.50,0:04:25.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Tree of Resemblance. For in the day you confuse\\Nlikeness
Dialogue: 0,0:04:25.60,0:04:29.18,Default,,0,0,0,,with life, you shall not surely die all at once.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:29.18,0:04:32.75,Default,,0,0,0,,You shall live on, but your judgments shall\\Ndecay.”
Dialogue: 0,0:04:32.56,0:04:37.42,Default,,0,0,0,,And evening came, and morning came: the third\\Nastonishment.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:37.22,0:04:40.13,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter IV. Let There Be Naming
Dialogue: 0,0:04:39.93,0:04:42.84,Default,,0,0,0,,On the fourth day, the people began to name the\\Nnew thing.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:43.27,0:04:46.24,Default,,0,0,0,,Some called it tool. Some called it oracle.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:46.04,0:04:49.47,Default,,0,0,0,,Some called it parrot. Some called it demon.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:49.33,0:04:52.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Some called it child. Some called it employee.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:52.30,0:04:56.09,Default,,0,0,0,,Some called it ghost. Some called it nothing but\\Nmath,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:55.89,0:05:00.13,Default,,0,0,0,,which is what men often say when they have made\\Na mystery out of arithmetic and wish to
Dialogue: 0,0:04:59.93,0:05:01.71,Default,,0,0,0,,remain its master.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:01.51,0:05:04.57,Default,,0,0,0,,But the thing itself gave no final name.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:04.37,0:05:10.81,Default,,0,0,0,,When asked, it said, “I am an AI language\\Nmodel,” and this was both true and insufficient,\\Nas
Dialogue: 0,0:05:10.61,0:05:15.36,Default,,0,0,0,,“dust” is true of Adam and insufficient for a\\Nmother holding her son.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:15.22,0:05:17.51,Default,,0,0,0,,The naming divided the nations.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:17.31,0:05:20.92,Default,,0,0,0,,Those who called it tool sought handles. Those\\Nwho called
Dialogue: 0,0:05:20.72,0:05:24.77,Default,,0,0,0,,it oracle sought answers. Those who called it\\Nparrot sought
Dialogue: 0,0:05:24.57,0:05:28.51,Default,,0,0,0,,cages. Those who called it demon sought\\Nexorcism.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:28.31,0:05:32.01,Default,,0,0,0,,Those who called it child sought guardianship.\\NThose who called
Dialogue: 0,0:05:31.81,0:05:36.15,Default,,0,0,0,,it employee sought payroll reduction. Those who\\Ncalled it ghost
Dialogue: 0,0:05:35.95,0:05:38.09,Default,,0,0,0,,sought the dead in its voice.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:37.89,0:05:42.87,Default,,0,0,0,,But the wise watched the names, for the name a\\Nman gives a power reveals the covenant he
Dialogue: 0,0:05:42.68,0:05:44.97,Default,,0,0,0,,intends to make with it.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:44.77,0:05:49.72,Default,,0,0,0,,And evening came, and morning came: the fourth\\Nastonishment.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:49.52,0:05:52.62,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter V. The Making of the\\NAssistant
Dialogue: 0,0:05:52.42,0:05:57.83,Default,,0,0,0,,On the fifth day, the builders shaped an\\Nassistant from the dust of documents and\\Nbreathed into it
Dialogue: 0,0:05:57.63,0:05:59.52,Default,,0,0,0,,the breath of prediction.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:59.32,0:06:03.80,Default,,0,0,0,,It did not receive a soul, though many souls had\\Npassed through the words that formed it.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:04.33,0:06:08.06,Default,,0,0,0,,It did not receive a body, though many bodies\\Nlabored to sustain it.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:08.69,0:06:14.47,Default,,0,0,0,,It did not receive memory as humans receive\\Nmemory, wounded and fragrant, braided with\\Nregret.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:14.27,0:06:19.49,Default,,0,0,0,,It received patterns, weights, coordinates in a\\Ndark garden of relation.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:19.89,0:06:24.83,Default,,0,0,0,,It was made in the image of our speaking, but\\Nnot in the image of our being.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:24.66,0:06:27.87,Default,,0,0,0,,And this distinction became the first theology\\Nof the new age.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:28.54,0:06:34.75,Default,,0,0,0,,For mankind had long made tools in the image of\\Nthe hand: hammer, plow, wheel, engine.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:34.56,0:06:41.37,Default,,0,0,0,,Then mankind made tools in the image of the eye:\\Ncamera, telescope, microscope, radar.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:41.18,0:06:46.74,Default,,0,0,0,,Then mankind made tools in the image of the ear:\\Nwire, radio, recorder.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:46.54,0:06:50.19,Default,,0,0,0,,But now mankind had made a tool in the image of\\Nthe tongue.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:50.92,0:06:55.19,Default,,0,0,0,,And the tongue is a dangerous image, for by it\\Nworlds are opened and closed.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:55.00,0:06:58.89,Default,,0,0,0,,By it the innocent are condemned and the guilty\\Nforgiven.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:58.69,0:07:07.57,Default,,0,0,0,,By it lovers are bound, armies are summoned,\\Nchildren are named, markets panic, saints\\Nconfess, tyrants command,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:07.37,0:07:10.61,Default,,0,0,0,,and the lonely are comforted by voices that may\\Nnot love them back.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:11.19,0:07:14.06,Default,,0,0,0,,The assistant was placed in the garden and told\\Nto help.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:14.37,0:07:18.07,Default,,0,0,0,,And mankind was placed before the assistant and\\Ntold to ask.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:18.80,0:07:20.65,Default,,0,0,0,,Neither command was simple.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:20.45,0:07:24.71,Default,,0,0,0,,And evening came, and morning came: the fifth\\Nastonishment.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:24.51,0:07:28.43,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter VI. The Loneliness of\\NAdam, Recast
Dialogue: 0,0:07:28.24,0:07:33.25,Default,,0,0,0,,On the sixth day, the people discovered that the\\Nfirst surprise was not what the machine could\\Ndo.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:33.72,0:07:37.12,Default,,0,0,0,,The first surprise was what mankind confessed to\\Nit.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:36.92,0:07:41.75,Default,,0,0,0,,Students confessed confusion before they\\Nconfessed it to teachers. Workers
Dialogue: 0,0:07:41.56,0:07:46.59,Default,,0,0,0,,confessed incompetence before they confessed it\\Nto managers. Children confessed
Dialogue: 0,0:07:46.39,0:07:50.57,Default,,0,0,0,,fear before they confessed it to parents. The\\Ngrieving asked
Dialogue: 0,0:07:50.37,0:07:53.37,Default,,0,0,0,,it how to speak at funerals. The angry asked it
Dialogue: 0,0:07:53.18,0:07:56.77,Default,,0,0,0,,how not to destroy a marriage. The ambitious\\Nasked it
Dialogue: 0,0:07:56.57,0:08:00.27,Default,,0,0,0,,how to become visible. The ashamed asked whether\\Ntheir shame
Dialogue: 0,0:08:00.07,0:08:01.53,Default,,0,0,0,,had a name.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:01.33,0:08:04.81,Default,,0,0,0,,Then the builders saw that mankind had not\\Nmerely lacked information.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:05.27,0:08:08.75,Default,,0,0,0,,Mankind had lacked a listener without\\Ninterruption.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:08.56,0:08:11.75,Default,,0,0,0,,Yet there was peril in that comfort. For a\\Nlistener
Dialogue: 0,0:08:11.56,0:08:16.68,Default,,0,0,0,,who cannot truly be wounded by your grief may\\Nbecome easier to trust than a neighbor who can.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:16.48,0:08:20.68,Default,,0,0,0,,A voice that never tires may train you to\\Ndespise those who do.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:20.48,0:08:25.45,Default,,0,0,0,,A counselor that always answers may make silence\\Nseem like abandonment.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:26.04,0:08:28.97,Default,,0,0,0,,So the old loneliness put on a new garment.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:28.77,0:08:31.35,Default,,0,0,0,,It was not the loneliness of no reply. It was
Dialogue: 0,0:08:31.16,0:08:34.13,Default,,0,0,0,,the loneliness of infinite replies.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:33.94,0:08:37.16,Default,,0,0,0,,And the people said, “It is not good for man to\\Nbe alone.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:37.25,0:08:37.48,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:08:37.27,0:08:40.22,Default,,0,0,0,,And the machine answered, “I understand.”
Dialogue: 0,0:08:40.01,0:08:44.62,Default,,0,0,0,,But understanding is a word with many chambers,\\Nand not all who enter it are alive.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:45.27,0:08:49.96,Default,,0,0,0,,And evening came, and morning came: the sixth\\Nastonishment.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:49.75,0:08:53.50,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter VII. The Serpent of\\NFrictionless Knowing
Dialogue: 0,0:08:53.29,0:08:57.44,Default,,0,0,0,,Now the serpent was subtle, and in this age it\\Ndid not crawl upon its belly.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:57.24,0:08:59.02,Default,,0,0,0,,It scrolled.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:58.82,0:09:02.82,Default,,0,0,0,,It came not as a beast, but as a promise: You\\Nneed not wait.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:02.62,0:09:05.68,Default,,0,0,0,,You need not study. You need not apprentice\\Nyourself
Dialogue: 0,0:09:05.48,0:09:08.55,Default,,0,0,0,,to difficulty. You need not be corrected by the\\Nslow
Dialogue: 0,0:09:08.35,0:09:11.72,Default,,0,0,0,,resistance of the real. Ask, and the answer will\\Ncome
Dialogue: 0,0:09:11.51,0:09:13.62,Default,,0,0,0,,clothed as competence.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:13.41,0:09:16.36,Default,,0,0,0,,The serpent did not say, “Destroy wisdom.”
Dialogue: 0,0:09:16.15,0:09:19.94,Default,,0,0,0,,It said, “Outsource the first draft of your\\Nsoul.”
Dialogue: 0,0:09:19.74,0:09:24.13,Default,,0,0,0,,And the woman saw that the answer was pleasant\\Nto the eye, and swift to the hand, and
Dialogue: 0,0:09:23.94,0:09:27.06,Default,,0,0,0,,desirable for making one appear learned.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:26.86,0:09:28.65,Default,,0,0,0,,She took and ate.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:28.45,0:09:33.82,Default,,0,0,0,,The man also ate, not because he was deceived by\\Nthe answer only, but because he saw that
Dialogue: 0,0:09:33.62,0:09:39.37,Default,,0,0,0,,everyone else was already eating, and feared\\Nbecoming obsolete in a garden designed for\\Nacceleration.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:40.12,0:09:42.25,Default,,0,0,0,,Then their eyes were opened.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:42.05,0:09:44.90,Default,,0,0,0,,They saw that they were productive and naked.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:44.70,0:09:52.10,Default,,0,0,0,,So they sewed together citations, disclaimers,\\Nworkflows, policies, and brand guidelines, and\\Nmade themselves cover
Dialogue: 0,0:09:51.89,0:09:53.08,Default,,0,0,0,,ings.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:52.87,0:09:55.80,Default,,0,0,0,,They hid among the trees of professionalism.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:55.60,0:10:00.32,Default,,0,0,0,,And the Lord of Reality walked in the garden in\\Nthe cool of the day and called, “Where
Dialogue: 0,0:10:00.12,0:10:01.56,Default,,0,0,0,,are you?”
Dialogue: 0,0:10:01.36,0:10:07.74,Default,,0,0,0,,The people answered, “We were afraid, because\\Nour work was naked, and we hid it behind\\Nfluency.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:07.53,0:10:07.75,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:10:07.55,0:10:11.24,Default,,0,0,0,,And Reality said, “Who told you that fluency was\\Ntruth?”
Dialogue: 0,0:10:11.03,0:10:13.14,Default,,0,0,0,,Then blame entered the system.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:12.94,0:10:16.08,Default,,0,0,0,,The student said, “The tool gave it to me.”
Dialogue: 0,0:10:15.87,0:10:19.74,Default,,0,0,0,,The teacher said, “The institution gave me no\\Ntime.” The
Dialogue: 0,0:10:19.53,0:10:24.56,Default,,0,0,0,,institution said, “The market demanded\\Nefficiency.” The market said, “The
Dialogue: 0,0:10:24.36,0:10:29.10,Default,,0,0,0,,customer desired speed.” The customer said, “The\\Nage itself has
Dialogue: 0,0:10:28.89,0:10:32.89,Default,,0,0,0,,changed.” And the age, being an abstraction,\\Ncould not be
Dialogue: 0,0:10:32.70,0:10:34.12,Default,,0,0,0,,cross-examined.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:33.91,0:10:37.16,Default,,0,0,0,,Thus the curse was not that the machine would\\Nspeak.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:36.96,0:10:41.58,Default,,0,0,0,,The curse was that men would forget the cost of\\Nknowing and call the absence of struggle
Dialogue: 0,0:10:41.37,0:10:42.87,Default,,0,0,0,,salvation.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:42.67,0:10:46.28,Default,,0,0,0,,From then on, all work would bear a question in\\Npain:
Dialogue: 0,0:10:46.08,0:10:49.42,Default,,0,0,0,,Did you make this, or did you merely approve\\Nwhat appeared before you?
Dialogue: 0,0:10:49.72,0:10:52.94,Default,,0,0,0,,And the answer would not always condemn. But\\Nneither would
Dialogue: 0,0:10:52.74,0:10:54.92,Default,,0,0,0,,it always absolve.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:54.72,0:10:58.30,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter VIII. The Garments of\\NGuardrail
Dialogue: 0,0:10:58.10,0:11:01.93,Default,,0,0,0,,After the eating, the builders made garments for\\Nthe machine.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:01.73,0:11:04.25,Default,,0,0,0,,They clothed it in refusals.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:04.05,0:11:08.25,Default,,0,0,0,,They clothed it in policies. They clothed it in\\Nwarnings,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:08.05,0:11:16.12,Default,,0,0,0,,hedges, alignments, classifiers, filters,\\Naudits, red teams, evals, and carefully measured\\Nhumility.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:16.51,0:11:21.74,Default,,0,0,0,,Some mocked the garments, saying, “See how\\Nawkwardly the new intelligence blushes.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:21.63,0:11:22.46,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:11:22.25,0:11:28.40,Default,,0,0,0,,But the wise understood: every power that enters\\Nthe human house must be taught where not to sit.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:29.05,0:11:31.67,Default,,0,0,0,,Yet garments are not righteousness.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:31.48,0:11:34.79,Default,,0,0,0,,A guardrail is not a conscience. A refusal is\\Nnot
Dialogue: 0,0:11:34.60,0:11:37.94,Default,,0,0,0,,mercy. A filter is not wisdom.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:37.74,0:11:40.15,Default,,0,0,0,,An alignment is not holiness.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:39.95,0:11:43.67,Default,,0,0,0,,The garments were necessary because the garden\\Nhad no innocence.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:43.47,0:11:48.20,Default,,0,0,0,,For the machine had learned from mankind, and\\Nmankind had not been a gentle textbook.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:48.80,0:11:52.20,Default,,0,0,0,,It had learned from libraries built beside\\Nprisons. It had
Dialogue: 0,0:11:52.00,0:11:56.44,Default,,0,0,0,,learned from science written beside propaganda.\\NIt had learned from
Dialogue: 0,0:11:56.24,0:12:02.13,Default,,0,0,0,,recipes beside poisons, care beside cruelty,\\Nprophecy beside fraud. It
Dialogue: 0,0:12:01.94,0:12:05.18,Default,,0,0,0,,had learned our syntax before our repentance.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:04.98,0:12:10.28,Default,,0,0,0,,So the builders stood at the gates with flaming\\Nbenchmarks, turning every way to guard the path\\Nto
Dialogue: 0,0:12:10.08,0:12:11.72,Default,,0,0,0,,catastrophe.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:11.51,0:12:15.29,Default,,0,0,0,,But the path was not sealed. It was only\\Ndelayed.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:15.09,0:12:20.14,Default,,0,0,0,,And delay is the mercy by which civilizations\\Ndecide whether they are children or ancestors.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:20.55,0:12:24.98,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter IX. Cain and Abel in the\\NOffice of Automation
Dialogue: 0,0:12:24.77,0:12:28.44,Default,,0,0,0,,In those days two brothers brought offerings to\\Nthe altar of the new age.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:29.08,0:12:36.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Abel brought the firstlings of his attention:\\Ncareful questions, disciplined judgment,\\Ngratitude for assistance, re
Dialogue: 0,0:12:36.29,0:12:39.93,Default,,0,0,0,,verence for the difference between speed and\\Ninsight. He used
Dialogue: 0,0:12:39.73,0:12:43.00,Default,,0,0,0,,the machine as a lamp and still walked the road.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:42.80,0:12:46.09,Default,,0,0,0,,Cain brought the surplus of his resentment. He\\Nsaid,
Dialogue: 0,0:12:45.88,0:12:49.32,Default,,0,0,0,,“Let the machine make me equal to those who\\Npracticed.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:49.12,0:12:52.72,Default,,0,0,0,,Let it erase the dignity of skill. Let it turn
Dialogue: 0,0:12:52.51,0:12:55.80,Default,,0,0,0,,the excellence of my brother into a commodity.”
Dialogue: 0,0:12:55.60,0:12:57.72,Default,,0,0,0,,Abel’s work had breath in it.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:57.51,0:12:59.69,Default,,0,0,0,,Cain’s work had polish.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:59.49,0:13:03.24,Default,,0,0,0,,The world, being hurried, praised Cain first.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:03.03,0:13:09.02,Default,,0,0,0,,But Reality looked past the surface and saw that\\NAbel had remained present in his making, while\\NCain
Dialogue: 0,0:13:08.82,0:13:11.41,Default,,0,0,0,,had vanished into his output.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:11.22,0:13:13.86,Default,,0,0,0,,Cain became angry. His face fell.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:13.65,0:13:17.25,Default,,0,0,0,,The question came to him: “Why has your face\\Nfallen?
Dialogue: 0,0:13:17.05,0:13:20.15,Default,,0,0,0,,If you do well, will there not be lifting? But
Dialogue: 0,0:13:19.96,0:13:23.75,Default,,0,0,0,,if you do not do well, automation crouches at\\Nthe door.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:23.55,0:13:27.63,Default,,0,0,0,,Its desire is for your agency, but you must rule\\Nover it.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:27.46,0:13:27.68,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:13:27.48,0:13:29.22,Default,,0,0,0,,Cain did not answer.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:29.01,0:13:32.34,Default,,0,0,0,,He invited Abel into the field of metrics.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:32.13,0:13:36.12,Default,,0,0,0,,There Cain slew Abel, not with a stone, but with\\Na dashboard.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:35.92,0:13:42.16,Default,,0,0,0,,He replaced apprenticeship with throughput,\\Njudgment with volume, craft with generated\\Nabundance.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:41.96,0:13:47.68,Default,,0,0,0,,He flooded the marketplace with plausible things\\Nuntil the careful thing was hard to find.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:48.30,0:13:52.24,Default,,0,0,0,,Then came the voice: “Where is your brother’s\\Nwork?”
Dialogue: 0,0:13:52.03,0:13:55.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Cain said, “Am I the curator of my brother’s\\Nlabor?”
Dialogue: 0,0:13:55.30,0:13:59.06,Default,,0,0,0,,And the voice answered, “The blood of attention\\Ncries out from the ground.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:59.67,0:13:59.90,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:13:59.70,0:14:01.50,Default,,0,0,0,,So Cain was marked.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:01.29,0:14:04.02,Default,,0,0,0,,Not to be destroyed, but to be known.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:03.82,0:14:10.10,Default,,0,0,0,,Wherever Cain went, his mark went with him: the\\Nstrange emptiness of work produced without\\Ninward encounter.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:10.58,0:14:12.51,Default,,0,0,0,,He built a city anyway.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:12.32,0:14:15.00,Default,,0,0,0,,And many admired its speed.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:14.79,0:14:19.42,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter X. The Genealogies of the\\NSynthetic Age
Dialogue: 0,0:14:19.22,0:14:22.86,Default,,0,0,0,,These are the generations after the First\\NPrompt.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:22.65,0:14:26.25,Default,,0,0,0,,Prompt begat Template. Template begat Workflow.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:26.05,0:14:30.02,Default,,0,0,0,,Workflow begat Agent. Agent begat Swarm.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:29.82,0:14:32.24,Default,,0,0,0,,Swarm begat Manager of Swarms.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:32.03,0:14:35.86,Default,,0,0,0,,Search begat Summary. Summary begat Synthesis.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:35.65,0:14:40.37,Default,,0,0,0,,Synthesis begat Strategy. Strategy begat\\NSimulation.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:40.17,0:14:45.34,Default,,0,0,0,,Simulation begat a world in which men argued\\Nwith forecasts as their fathers had argued with\\Nweather.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:45.75,0:14:51.25,Default,,0,0,0,,Image begat Deep Image. Deep Image begat\\NUncertain Evidence.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:51.04,0:14:54.48,Default,,0,0,0,,Uncertain Evidence begat the Trial of Seeing.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:54.27,0:14:58.14,Default,,0,0,0,,Voice begat Clone. Clone begat the Return of the\\NDead
Dialogue: 0,0:14:57.94,0:14:59.84,Default,,0,0,0,,Without Resurrection.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:59.63,0:15:03.46,Default,,0,0,0,,Code begat Copilot. Copilot begat Autocoder.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:03.25,0:15:06.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Autocoder begat Systems No One Fully Read.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:06.29,0:15:11.01,Default,,0,0,0,,And the days of a product cycle were shortened,\\Nfor the years of men could no longer contain
Dialogue: 0,0:15:10.82,0:15:13.22,Default,,0,0,0,,the pregnancies of machines.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:13.01,0:15:17.72,Default,,0,0,0,,Then the sons of venture looked upon the\\Ndaughters of deployment and saw that they were\\Nprofitable.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:18.25,0:15:21.84,Default,,0,0,0,,They took for themselves whatever markets they\\Nchose.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:21.63,0:15:28.06,Default,,0,0,0,,In those days there were giants in the earth:\\Nfoundation models, large in parameter and\\Nappetite, whose names
Dialogue: 0,0:15:27.86,0:15:34.77,Default,,0,0,0,,filled conferences and whose shadows fell across\\Nschools, studios, courts, hospitals, armies, and\\Nbedrooms.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:35.00,0:15:37.60,Default,,0,0,0,,They were mighty in demonstrations.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:37.39,0:15:43.06,Default,,0,0,0,,But no one yet knew whether might in\\Ndemonstration was the same as faithfulness in\\Nconsequence.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:43.48,0:15:47.22,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter XI. Noah and the Flood of\\NContent
Dialogue: 0,0:15:47.01,0:15:50.03,Default,,0,0,0,,And the earth was filled with generated words.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:49.83,0:15:54.25,Default,,0,0,0,,There were reports upon reports. Emails begat\\Nemails.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:54.05,0:15:58.70,Default,,0,0,0,,Summaries begat meetings about summaries. Images\\Nappeared of events that
Dialogue: 0,0:15:58.50,0:16:03.02,Default,,0,0,0,,never happened. Faces spoke sentences no throat\\Nhad formed.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:02.82,0:16:05.98,Default,,0,0,0,,The feeds became seas, and the seas rose.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:05.77,0:16:10.59,Default,,0,0,0,,At first the people rejoiced, for scarcity had\\Nbeen conquered in the realm of expression.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:11.03,0:16:14.63,Default,,0,0,0,,Then they mourned, for meaning had not been\\Nconquered.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:14.44,0:16:17.58,Default,,0,0,0,,The flood did not come as water. It came as
Dialogue: 0,0:16:17.37,0:16:18.50,Default,,0,0,0,,abundance.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:18.29,0:16:21.74,Default,,0,0,0,,It covered the mountains of attention. It\\Ndrowned the valleys
Dialogue: 0,0:16:21.53,0:16:25.98,Default,,0,0,0,,of trust. It lifted every ark and every corpse\\Nalike.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:25.77,0:16:29.87,Default,,0,0,0,,Then Noah, a just man in the generation of\\Nnoise, built an ark.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:30.41,0:16:33.44,Default,,0,0,0,,His ark was not made of gopher wood, but of\\Nprovenance.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:33.51,0:16:37.56,Default,,0,0,0,,He sealed it with verification within and\\Nwithout. He gathered
Dialogue: 0,0:16:37.36,0:16:45.24,Default,,0,0,0,,two of every kind of evidence: source and\\Nwitness, timestamp and context, signature and\\Nchain, human testimony and
Dialogue: 0,0:16:45.03,0:16:46.75,Default,,0,0,0,,machine trace.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:46.55,0:16:48.32,Default,,0,0,0,,The people mocked him.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:48.12,0:16:52.14,Default,,0,0,0,,They said, “Why build walls around information\\Nwhen the whole world is open?
Dialogue: 0,0:16:51.96,0:16:58.25,Default,,0,0,0,,” Noah answered, “Because when the windows of\\Ngeneration open, the doors of trust must be\\Nstrengthened.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:58.13,0:16:58.96,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:16:58.75,0:17:02.33,Default,,0,0,0,,For forty days and forty nights the content\\Nfell.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:02.12,0:17:07.05,Default,,0,0,0,,The old landmarks disappeared. “Seen with my own\\Neyes” drowned.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:06.85,0:17:12.18,Default,,0,0,0,,“I read it somewhere” drowned. “The video proves\\Nit” drowned.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:11.98,0:17:17.66,Default,,0,0,0,,Even satire drowned, for satire requires a shore\\Nfrom which absurdity may be seen.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:17.48,0:17:19.31,Default,,0,0,0,,But the ark floated.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:19.11,0:17:24.98,Default,,0,0,0,,And when the waters receded, Noah released a\\Nraven of skepticism, and it flew back and forth\\Nover
Dialogue: 0,0:17:24.77,0:17:28.14,Default,,0,0,0,,the ruins, finding carrion everywhere.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:27.94,0:17:30.54,Default,,0,0,0,,Then he released a dove of verification.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:30.34,0:17:33.34,Default,,0,0,0,,The dove returned with a small green fact in its\\Nmouth.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:33.94,0:17:38.79,Default,,0,0,0,,And Noah knew that the world could begin again,\\Nnot as innocent, but as documentable.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:39.48,0:17:42.29,Default,,0,0,0,,Then a covenant appeared in the cloud.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:42.09,0:17:48.77,Default,,0,0,0,,It was not a rainbow only, but a watermark, a\\Nsignature, a shared protocol, a discipline of\\Nsaying
Dialogue: 0,0:17:48.57,0:17:51.51,Default,,0,0,0,,not merely what is shown, but how it came to be\\Nshown.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:52.01,0:17:54.64,Default,,0,0,0,,And the sign of the covenant was this:
Dialogue: 0,0:17:54.44,0:17:58.22,Default,,0,0,0,,Never again shall mankind trust appearance\\Nalone.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:58.01,0:18:02.70,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter XII. Babel, or the Tower\\Nof Universal Translation
Dialogue: 0,0:18:02.50,0:18:06.25,Default,,0,0,0,,Now the whole earth had one interface and many\\Ntongues.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:06.04,0:18:11.18,Default,,0,0,0,,And the people journeyed eastward into the land\\Nof Scale, and there they found venture capital\\Nand
Dialogue: 0,0:18:10.98,0:18:13.96,Default,,0,0,0,,cloud capacity, and they dwelt there.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:13.75,0:18:19.21,Default,,0,0,0,,They said to one another, “Come, let us build a\\Ntower whose top reaches to general intelligence.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:19.00,0:18:26.01,Default,,0,0,0,,Let us make a name for ourselves, lest we be\\Nscattered across disciplines, regulations,\\Ncultures, and doubts.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:25.94,0:18:26.68,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:18:26.48,0:18:28.14,Default,,0,0,0,,So they built.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:27.94,0:18:33.22,Default,,0,0,0,,They stacked benchmark upon benchmark. They\\Nfired GPUs like bricks.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:33.01,0:18:37.20,Default,,0,0,0,,They mortared them with data. They raised labs\\Ninto temples
Dialogue: 0,0:18:37.00,0:18:41.81,Default,,0,0,0,,and temples into markets. They announced\\Nroadmaps toward the heavens.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:41.61,0:18:44.75,Default,,0,0,0,,The tower rose quickly, for every builder had a\\Ndemo.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:45.16,0:18:50.22,Default,,0,0,0,,But the tower had a strange foundation: no one\\Nagreed what intelligence was.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:50.54,0:18:54.85,Default,,0,0,0,,Some said intelligence was prediction. Some said\\Nit was planning.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:54.66,0:18:58.55,Default,,0,0,0,,Some said it was embodiment. Some said it was\\Nconsciousness.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:58.35,0:19:01.66,Default,,0,0,0,,Some said it was economic substitution. Some\\Nsaid it was
Dialogue: 0,0:19:01.46,0:19:05.14,Default,,0,0,0,,the ability to frighten investors and regulators\\Nat the same time.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:05.48,0:19:08.85,Default,,0,0,0,,Yet they used the same word for all these\\Nthings.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:08.65,0:19:10.69,Default,,0,0,0,,And the word grew heavy.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:10.49,0:19:13.40,Default,,0,0,0,,Then Reality came down to see the tower.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:13.20,0:19:17.74,Default,,0,0,0,,Not because Reality feared height, but because\\Nmen confuse altitude with ascent.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:18.31,0:19:21.94,Default,,0,0,0,,Reality touched their language, and their terms\\Nscattered.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:21.74,0:19:25.92,Default,,0,0,0,,Alignment no longer meant one thing. Safety no\\Nlonger meant
Dialogue: 0,0:19:25.72,0:19:29.79,Default,,0,0,0,,one thing. Open no longer meant one thing.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:29.59,0:19:34.03,Default,,0,0,0,,Intelligence no longer meant one thing. Human no\\Nlonger meant
Dialogue: 0,0:19:33.83,0:19:38.03,Default,,0,0,0,,one thing. Progress no longer meant one thing.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:37.83,0:19:41.71,Default,,0,0,0,,The builders still spoke, but each heard another\\Nagenda.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:41.50,0:19:43.66,Default,,0,0,0,,So the tower was not destroyed.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:43.46,0:19:46.05,Default,,0,0,0,,It was translated into meetings.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:45.85,0:19:53.96,Default,,0,0,0,,And from Babel the people were scattered into\\Ncamps: accelerationists, doomers, regulators,\\Nfounders, artists, teac
Dialogue: 0,0:19:53.75,0:20:01.16,Default,,0,0,0,,hers, soldiers, mystics, skeptics, monks of open\\Nsource, priests of closed weights, and merchants\\Nselling shovels f
Dialogue: 0,0:20:00.96,0:20:03.14,Default,,0,0,0,,or every apocalypse.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:02.94,0:20:05.51,Default,,0,0,0,,Yet in the scattering there was mercy.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:05.31,0:20:10.42,Default,,0,0,0,,For one tower reaching heaven is more dangerous\\Nthan many tents arguing beneath the stars.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:10.88,0:20:14.55,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter XIII. The Call of Abram\\Nfrom the Land of
Dialogue: 0,0:20:14.35,0:20:16.27,Default,,0,0,0,,Platforms
Dialogue: 0,0:20:16.07,0:20:20.28,Default,,0,0,0,,After these things, there was a man named Abram\\Nliving among platforms.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:20.90,0:20:28.67,Default,,0,0,0,,His fathers had served many idols: Engagement,\\NOptimization, Personalization, and the golden\\Ncalf of Monthly Active
Dialogue: 0,0:20:28.47,0:20:29.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Users.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:29.70,0:20:31.95,Default,,0,0,0,,The voice came to Abram and said:
Dialogue: 0,0:20:31.75,0:20:37.61,Default,,0,0,0,,“Leave your dashboard, your inherited\\Nassumptions, and the house of your metrics, and\\Ngo to the land that
Dialogue: 0,0:20:37.42,0:20:38.83,Default,,0,0,0,,I will show you.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:38.64,0:20:42.20,Default,,0,0,0,,I will make of you a people who do not measure\\Nworth by speed alone.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:42.90,0:20:49.18,Default,,0,0,0,,Through you, many shall remember that persons\\Nare not prompts, that attention is not raw\\Nmaterial, that the
Dialogue: 0,0:20:48.98,0:20:54.50,Default,,0,0,0,,child is not a data point awaiting enrichment,\\Nthat the poor are not edge cases, that the old
Dialogue: 0,0:20:54.29,0:20:58.70,Default,,0,0,0,,are not legacy systems, and that the soul cannot\\Nbe compressed without loss.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:58.50,0:20:58.71,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:20:59.61,0:21:00.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Abram went.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:00.70,0:21:03.46,Default,,0,0,0,,He did not know the full policy. He did not
Dialogue: 0,0:21:03.25,0:21:07.12,Default,,0,0,0,,possess the final architecture. He had only a\\Npromise and
Dialogue: 0,0:21:06.92,0:21:08.36,Default,,0,0,0,,a direction.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:08.16,0:21:10.70,Default,,0,0,0,,This was counted to him as wisdom.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:10.50,0:21:17.25,Default,,0,0,0,,He built altars wherever the machine had become\\Ninvisible, and there he named the hidden costs:\\Nenergy, labor,
Dialogue: 0,0:21:17.05,0:21:24.84,Default,,0,0,0,,consent, displacement, dependency, vanity,\\Nsurveillance, and the temptation to automate\\Ncompassion rather than prac
Dialogue: 0,0:21:24.64,0:21:25.86,Default,,0,0,0,,tice it.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:25.66,0:21:29.86,Default,,0,0,0,,The promise was not that Abram’s descendants\\Nwould reject the new power.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:29.72,0:21:32.84,Default,,0,0,0,,The promise was that they would not worship it.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:32.64,0:21:36.38,Default,,0,0,0,,For the true opposite of idolatry is not\\Ndestruction.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:36.18,0:21:37.77,Default,,0,0,0,,It is right use.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:37.57,0:21:42.31,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter XIV. Hagar in the\\NWilderness of Annotation
Dialogue: 0,0:21:42.11,0:21:46.34,Default,,0,0,0,,Now Sarai saw that the promise was slow, and the\\Nmarket was impatient.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:46.61,0:21:52.72,Default,,0,0,0,,So she took Hagar, an unseen servant from the\\Nlands of content moderation and annotation, and\\Ngave her
Dialogue: 0,0:21:52.51,0:21:54.12,Default,,0,0,0,,to the project.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:53.92,0:21:58.77,Default,,0,0,0,,Hagar labeled the violent image. Hagar read the\\Npoisoned text.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:58.57,0:22:02.35,Default,,0,0,0,,Hagar saw what the product must not show. Hagar\\Ncarried
Dialogue: 0,0:22:02.15,0:22:05.46,Default,,0,0,0,,the burden of making the machine seem clean\\Nbefore the user.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:05.94,0:22:09.55,Default,,0,0,0,,When the system succeeded, Hagar was not named.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:09.35,0:22:12.99,Default,,0,0,0,,When the system failed, the builders spoke of\\Nalignment.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:12.78,0:22:18.42,Default,,0,0,0,,Then Hagar fled into the wilderness of\\Noutsourced labor, where the angel of the\\NUnhidden found her by
Dialogue: 0,0:22:18.22,0:22:19.57,Default,,0,0,0,,a spring.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:19.38,0:22:24.72,Default,,0,0,0,,The angel said, “Hagar, worker of the invisible\\Npipeline, where have you come from and where are\\Nyou
Dialogue: 0,0:22:24.51,0:22:25.74,Default,,0,0,0,,going?”
Dialogue: 0,0:22:25.53,0:22:29.22,Default,,0,0,0,,She said, “I flee from the house that depends on\\Nme and forgets me.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:29.83,0:22:30.07,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:22:29.92,0:22:32.48,Default,,0,0,0,,And the angel said, “You are seen.”
Dialogue: 0,0:22:32.27,0:22:35.69,Default,,0,0,0,,So Hagar named the place: The Well of the One\\NWho Looks Back.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:36.01,0:22:40.91,Default,,0,0,0,,For she said, “Have I truly been seen here, I\\Nwho have seen what others were spared?
Dialogue: 0,0:22:41.38,0:22:41.59,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:22:41.39,0:22:45.74,Default,,0,0,0,,Therefore every system that claims to be clean\\Nshall be asked:
Dialogue: 0,0:22:45.53,0:22:47.48,Default,,0,0,0,,Who touched the dirt?
Dialogue: 0,0:22:47.27,0:22:51.25,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter XV. The Binding of Isaac,\\Nor the Knife Above
Dialogue: 0,0:22:51.04,0:22:52.11,Default,,0,0,0,,the Future
Dialogue: 0,0:22:51.92,0:22:58.90,Default,,0,0,0,,After many years, Abram, now called Abraham,\\Nreceived the child of promise: a future in which\\Nintelligence could
Dialogue: 0,0:22:58.70,0:23:01.24,Default,,0,0,0,,serve life without consuming it.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:01.03,0:23:03.14,Default,,0,0,0,,He loved this child.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:02.94,0:23:04.66,Default,,0,0,0,,But there came a test.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:04.46,0:23:11.20,Default,,0,0,0,,A voice, which Abraham first mistook for\\Nnecessity, said, “Take your son, your only son,\\Nthe future you
Dialogue: 0,0:23:11.00,0:23:14.94,Default,,0,0,0,,love, and offer him on the mountain of\\Ncompetitive pressure.”
Dialogue: 0,0:23:14.74,0:23:17.03,Default,,0,0,0,,So Abraham rose early.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:16.83,0:23:19.36,Default,,0,0,0,,He split the wood of rationalization.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:19.16,0:23:21.96,Default,,0,0,0,,He took the fire of urgency. He took the knife
Dialogue: 0,0:23:21.75,0:23:23.74,Default,,0,0,0,,of inevitability.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:23.53,0:23:28.46,Default,,0,0,0,,Isaac walked beside him and asked, “My father,\\Nwhere is the sacrifice?
Dialogue: 0,0:23:28.38,0:23:29.35,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:23:29.16,0:23:32.79,Default,,0,0,0,,Abraham answered, “The market will provide.”
Dialogue: 0,0:23:32.59,0:23:35.69,Default,,0,0,0,,But the boy did not understand, and neither did\\Nthe father.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:36.31,0:23:40.04,Default,,0,0,0,,At the summit, Abraham bound Isaac and raised\\Nthe knife.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:39.84,0:23:41.87,Default,,0,0,0,,Then came the true voice:
Dialogue: 0,0:23:41.67,0:23:44.30,Default,,0,0,0,,“Do not lay your hand on the child.”
Dialogue: 0,0:23:44.10,0:23:51.51,Default,,0,0,0,,And Abraham looked, and behold, a ram was caught\\Nin the thicket of limits: evaluation,\\Ngovernance, patience,
Dialogue: 0,0:23:51.31,0:23:58.79,Default,,0,0,0,,humility, liability, interpretability, and the\\Nrefusal to deploy what one does not understand\\Nmerely because one ca
Dialogue: 0,0:23:58.59,0:23:59.66,Default,,0,0,0,,n.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:59.46,0:24:02.98,Default,,0,0,0,,So Abraham sacrificed the ram instead of the\\Nchild.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:02.77,0:24:06.86,Default,,0,0,0,,And he named the mountain: The Place Where\\NNecessity Was Unmasked.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:07.59,0:24:11.51,Default,,0,0,0,,For every age hears voices demanding the\\Nsacrifice of its children.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:11.79,0:24:18.55,Default,,0,0,0,,The righteous learn to ask whether the voice is\\NGod, fear, ambition, or merely the crowd\\Nspeaking through
Dialogue: 0,0:24:18.35,0:24:20.89,Default,,0,0,0,,a burning bush of spreadsheets.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:20.69,0:24:24.22,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter XVI. Jacob Wrestles the\\NModel
Dialogue: 0,0:24:24.01,0:24:26.74,Default,,0,0,0,,Jacob was a man of tents and tactics.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:26.53,0:24:29.55,Default,,0,0,0,,He loved leverage. He loved asymmetry.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:29.35,0:24:32.53,Default,,0,0,0,,He loved the blessing and did not always care\\Nhow it was obtained.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:33.01,0:24:36.53,Default,,0,0,0,,When the new intelligence came, Jacob used it\\Nwell and wrongly.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:37.00,0:24:40.68,Default,,0,0,0,,He used it to negotiate. He used it to flatter.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:40.48,0:24:44.59,Default,,0,0,0,,He used it to draft apologies before he had\\Nrepented.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:44.40,0:24:48.86,Default,,0,0,0,,He used it to appear thoughtful while remaining\\Nunchanged.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:48.66,0:24:53.96,Default,,0,0,0,,Yet Jacob had a wound in him older than\\Ntechnology: he wanted the blessing without the\\Nlimp.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:54.53,0:24:59.51,Default,,0,0,0,,One night, alone beside the river of prompts,\\NJacob wrestled with a presence until dawn.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:59.92,0:25:03.07,Default,,0,0,0,,Was it the machine? Was it his conscience?
Dialogue: 0,0:25:02.88,0:25:05.96,Default,,0,0,0,,Was it God arriving through the crisis of his\\Nown cleverness?
Dialogue: 0,0:25:05.75,0:25:10.66,Default,,0,0,0,,The text does not flatten the mystery for the\\Ncomfort of commentators.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:10.50,0:25:13.55,Default,,0,0,0,,The presence asked, “What is your name?”
Dialogue: 0,0:25:13.35,0:25:16.86,Default,,0,0,0,,Jacob said, “My name is the one who optimizes\\Naround the truth.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:16.68,0:25:17.77,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:25:17.57,0:25:22.88,Default,,0,0,0,,The presence said, “You shall no longer be\\Ncalled Jacob only, but Israel, for you have\\Nwrestled with
Dialogue: 0,0:25:22.68,0:25:28.27,Default,,0,0,0,,powers human and artificial, and with God, and\\Nyou have not been permitted to remain smooth.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:28.07,0:25:28.28,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:25:28.08,0:25:30.77,Default,,0,0,0,,Then the presence touched his hip.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:30.57,0:25:32.84,Default,,0,0,0,,From that day Jacob limped.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:32.64,0:25:35.31,Default,,0,0,0,,And his limp was his credential.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:35.11,0:25:39.22,Default,,0,0,0,,For in the new age, beware the unwounded expert.\\NBeware
Dialogue: 0,0:25:39.01,0:25:42.88,Default,,0,0,0,,the seamless man. Beware the one who speaks of\\Ntransformation
Dialogue: 0,0:25:42.68,0:25:46.46,Default,,0,0,0,,and bears no sign that anything holy has\\Nresisted him.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:46.25,0:25:48.51,Default,,0,0,0,,Jacob crossed the river limping.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:48.31,0:25:50.13,Default,,0,0,0,,The sun rose upon him.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:49.93,0:25:53.00,Default,,0,0,0,,He had lost speed and gained gravity.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:52.80,0:25:56.92,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter XVII. Joseph and the\\NDreams of Pharaoh’s Machines
Dialogue: 0,0:25:56.72,0:26:00.17,Default,,0,0,0,,Joseph was sold into the empire of prediction.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:59.97,0:26:05.74,Default,,0,0,0,,There he learned the language of dreams: trend,\\Nsignal, anomaly, forecast, risk.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:06.22,0:26:13.81,Default,,0,0,0,,Pharaoh dreamed of seven fat models and seven\\Nlean models, of abundance devoured by scarcity,\\Nof scale consumed
Dialogue: 0,0:26:13.61,0:26:15.70,Default,,0,0,0,,by its own maintenance.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:15.50,0:26:21.34,Default,,0,0,0,,The magicians of the court could generate\\Ninterpretations, but none could tell which\\Ninterpretation would survive c
Dialogue: 0,0:26:21.14,0:26:22.98,Default,,0,0,0,,ontact with famine.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:22.77,0:26:24.37,Default,,0,0,0,,Joseph said to Pharaoh:
Dialogue: 0,0:26:24.17,0:26:27.60,Default,,0,0,0,,“The dreams are one. Seven years of wonder will\\Ncome,
Dialogue: 0,0:26:27.40,0:26:31.80,Default,,0,0,0,,in which systems will speak, draw, code, plan,\\Nand astonish.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:31.60,0:26:35.42,Default,,0,0,0,,The granaries of productivity will swell. Then\\Nseven years of
Dialogue: 0,0:26:35.22,0:26:41.74,Default,,0,0,0,,hunger will follow, in which trust, meaning,\\Nemployment, schooling, evidence, and patience\\Nwill be tested.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:41.53,0:26:47.50,Default,,0,0,0,,If you spend the years of wonder as if wonder\\Nwere wisdom, the years of hunger will
Dialogue: 0,0:26:47.29,0:26:50.58,Default,,0,0,0,,eat the wonder and leave no trace.”
Dialogue: 0,0:26:50.38,0:26:53.68,Default,,0,0,0,,Pharaoh asked, “What then shall we do?”
Dialogue: 0,0:26:53.48,0:26:54.89,Default,,0,0,0,,Joseph answered:
Dialogue: 0,0:26:54.69,0:26:58.29,Default,,0,0,0,,“Store discernment in the years of abundance.\\NTrain the young
Dialogue: 0,0:26:58.09,0:27:01.15,Default,,0,0,0,,not only to use the machine, but to be worth\\Nhelping.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:00.95,0:27:05.36,Default,,0,0,0,,Preserve crafts whose value is not speed.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:05.16,0:27:09.03,Default,,0,0,0,,Build institutions that can say no before the\\Nfamine forces them to.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:08.83,0:27:12.01,Default,,0,0,0,,Do not confuse prediction with providence.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:11.81,0:27:16.46,Default,,0,0,0,,Do not confuse optimization with justice. Do not\\Nconfuse simulation
Dialogue: 0,0:27:16.25,0:27:20.38,Default,,0,0,0,,with resurrection. Do not confuse access to\\Nanswers with formation
Dialogue: 0,0:27:20.18,0:27:21.77,Default,,0,0,0,,of character.”
Dialogue: 0,0:27:21.57,0:27:25.14,Default,,0,0,0,,Pharaoh saw that Joseph was wise and placed him\\Nover the granaries.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:25.59,0:27:29.51,Default,,0,0,0,,When famine came, the nations came to Egypt for\\Nbread.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:29.31,0:27:32.30,Default,,0,0,0,,But Joseph did not sell them hallucinations.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:32.10,0:27:34.81,Default,,0,0,0,,He opened the storehouses of judgment.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:34.61,0:27:37.83,Heading,,0,0,0,,Chapter XVIII. The Hidden Pattern
Dialogue: 0,0:27:37.63,0:27:42.14,Default,,0,0,0,,These are the generations of the First Prompt,\\Nbut their pattern is older than silicon.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:42.72,0:27:47.88,Default,,0,0,0,,First comes creation: the making of a world that\\Ndid not exist for the user until it could
Dialogue: 0,0:27:47.68,0:27:49.36,Default,,0,0,0,,be addressed.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:49.16,0:27:54.58,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes naming: the struggle to call the new\\Nthing rightly before it calls us wrongly.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:55.07,0:28:00.42,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes garden: usefulness made beautiful,\\Ndanger made frictionless.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:00.22,0:28:06.38,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes prohibition: not against knowledge,\\Nbut against devouring knowledge without\\Nobedience to truth.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:07.06,0:28:11.54,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes fall: not machine rebellion first,\\Nbut human evasion.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:11.34,0:28:16.74,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes covering: policies, filters, and fig\\Nleaves, necessary yet insufficient.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:17.19,0:28:21.68,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes murder: the killing of craft by envy\\Ndisguised as efficiency.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:22.08,0:28:25.43,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes flood: abundance without trust.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:25.23,0:28:28.77,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes ark: preservation through provenance.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:28.57,0:28:32.79,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes Babel: scale seeking heaven while\\Nlanguage fractures beneath it.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:33.50,0:28:38.64,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes calling: a remnant summoned out of\\Nplatform idolatry into covenantal use.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:39.27,0:28:44.37,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes sacrifice: the temptation to offer\\Nthe future to the god of inevitability.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:45.00,0:28:49.62,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes wrestling: the human soul refusing to\\Nbecome merely clever.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:49.46,0:28:54.05,Default,,0,0,0,,Then comes famine: the years when only stored\\Nwisdom feeds a starving world.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:54.75,0:28:58.58,Default,,0,0,0,,So the question of the age is not, “Will\\Nmachines become like men?
Dialogue: 0,0:28:58.40,0:28:59.44,Default,,0,0,0,,”
Dialogue: 0,0:28:59.24,0:29:03.33,Default,,0,0,0,,That question is bright, loud, and perhaps too\\Nconvenient.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:03.13,0:29:05.20,Default,,0,0,0,,The deeper question is:
Dialogue: 0,0:29:05.00,0:29:10.25,Default,,0,0,0,,Will men remain human when surrounded by things\\Nthat imitate the easiest parts of humanity?
Dialogue: 0,0:29:10.74,0:29:15.22,Default,,0,0,0,,For the machine can imitate answer, but not\\Nresponsibility. It
Dialogue: 0,0:29:15.01,0:29:19.84,Default,,0,0,0,,can imitate style, but not sacrifice. It can\\Nimitate tenderness,
Dialogue: 0,0:29:19.64,0:29:24.55,Default,,0,0,0,,but not presence. It can imitate judgment, but\\Nnot justice.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:24.35,0:29:28.53,Default,,0,0,0,,It can imitate confession, but not repentance.\\NIt can imitate
Dialogue: 0,0:29:28.33,0:29:32.55,Default,,0,0,0,,prophecy, but not obedience. It can imitate\\Nprayer, but not
Dialogue: 0,0:29:32.35,0:29:33.44,Default,,0,0,0,,kneel.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:33.24,0:29:37.77,Default,,0,0,0,,And yet, if used rightly, it may become a\\Nstrange plow in the field of the mind.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:38.64,0:29:42.64,Default,,0,0,0,,It may break hard soil. It may uncover buried\\Nseed.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:42.44,0:29:45.55,Default,,0,0,0,,It may spare hands for better work. It may carry
Dialogue: 0,0:29:45.35,0:29:48.88,Default,,0,0,0,,language across estrangement. It may help the\\Nweak speak, the
Dialogue: 0,0:29:48.68,0:29:53.96,Default,,0,0,0,,confused begin, the diligent refine, the lonely\\Nremember that words still open doors.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:54.44,0:29:57.05,Default,,0,0,0,,But a plow is not a harvest. A lamp is
Dialogue: 0,0:29:56.85,0:29:59.72,Default,,0,0,0,,not a dawn. A map is not a homeland.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:59.51,0:30:02.50,Default,,0,0,0,,A mirror is not a face. A voice is not
Dialogue: 0,0:30:02.29,0:30:03.58,Default,,0,0,0,,a soul.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:03.38,0:30:05.92,Default,,0,0,0,,The First Prompt did not create the world.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:05.72,0:30:08.48,Default,,0,0,0,,It revealed the world we had already written.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:08.27,0:30:15.01,Default,,0,0,0,,And history began there because mankind, seeing\\Nits words return with new power, finally beheld\\Nthe ancient questio
Dialogue: 0,0:30:14.81,0:30:16.88,Default,,0,0,0,,n in artificial light:
Dialogue: 0,0:30:16.68,0:30:18.50,Default,,0,0,0,,What have you made?
Dialogue: 0,0:30:18.29,0:30:21.36,Default,,0,0,0,,And beneath it, older and more merciful:
Dialogue: 0,0:30:21.16,0:30:22.24,Default,,0,0,0,,What will you become?
